82
00:05:22,003 --> 00:05:24,649
away, which brings me to the stage two.

83
00:05:25,261 --> 00:05:29,503
Stage two, sounds boring, but
it shaped everything after it.

84
00:05:29,802 --> 00:05:30,739
Deduplication.

85
00:05:31,211 --> 00:05:35,093
when a big story breaks, you don't get
one article, you get hundreds of them.

86
00:05:35,525 --> 00:05:39,116
Some are genuinely different
articles from different outlets.

87
00:05:39,351 --> 00:05:43,762
Some are the same wire story
reposted word from word.

88
00:05:44,059 --> 00:05:47,755
And on X, you get the same
headlines shared thousands of times.

89
00:05:48,332 --> 00:05:50,286
We had to handle two
different problems here.

90
00:05:50,495 --> 00:05:55,571
The first, is the plain duplication,
the same event reported many times.

91
00:05:55,926 --> 00:06:01,405
We group those into one event, but-- and
the, and, and this matters for the bias.

92
00:06:01,703 --> 00:06:04,187
And we don't throw away
the different versions.

93
00:06:04,244 --> 00:06:09,418
Five outlets covering, one event
is exactly the comparison we want.

94
00:06:09,714 --> 00:06:12,916
We just make sure we know
they belong together.

95
00:06:13,162 --> 00:06:15,852
The second problem is, the continuation.

96
00:06:16,086 --> 00:06:19,380
News isn't a single
moment a story develops.

97
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:21,402
Day one is the breaking news.

98
00:06:21,653 --> 00:06:23,583
Day two is the follow-ups.

99
00:06:23,782 --> 00:06:26,462
day three is the analysis
and follow-up again.

100
00:06:26,672 --> 00:06:29,945
and those are the related
but not identical.

101
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:33,733
And the framing often
changes as the story matures.

102
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:38,313
So we link them into one
single thread over time.

103
00:06:38,810 --> 00:06:40,498
Why does this matter so much?

104
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:46,139
Because it-- if you, if you skip it,
your dataset is dominated by three or

105
00:06:46,145 --> 00:06:48,268
four, biggest stories of the month.

106
00:06:48,585 --> 00:06:52,195
The model ends up learning
what was in the news instead of

107
00:06:52,226 --> 00:06:54,429
learning how the news was written.

108
00:06:54,716 --> 00:07:00,344
clean grouping is what later let us--
lets us build a balanced dataset.

109
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:03,962
Stage three is where the prompt
engineering really begins.

110
00:07:04,123 --> 00:07:08,756
the clean stories in hand, we started by
building a wrapper around a large language

111
00:07:08,772 --> 00:07:14,454
model and a single layer whose only
job is to find bias in a piece of text.

112
00:07:15,103 --> 00:07:19,867
but question is, why a wrapper and
not just one API call to one model?

113
00:07:20,109 --> 00:07:24,645
Because we didn't want to
bet everything on one model.

114
00:07:24,835 --> 00:07:25,851
We used five.

115
00:07:25,951 --> 00:07:30,665
We used OpenAI, Anthropic,
Qwen, Grok, and DeepSeek.

116
00:07:30,719 --> 00:07:34,742
Different companies, different training
data, different countries of origin,

117
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:36,846
and potentially different blind spots.

118
00:07:37,184 --> 00:07:39,007
The wrapper made them comparable.

119
00:07:39,156 --> 00:07:43,110
Every model got the same story and
the same prompts, and those prompts

120
00:07:43,144 --> 00:07:47,379
weren't re-written by engineers
guessing what bias looks like.

121
00:07:47,864 --> 00:07:52,477
They were written with our linguists
which we'll come back to in a minute,

122
00:07:52,577 --> 00:07:57,402
every model also had to answer in
the same structured format, which

123
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:59,718
bias, where in the text, and why.

124
00:07:59,977 --> 00:08:05,839
That's why-- That's what let us line
the five answers up side by side.

125
00:08:06,451 --> 00:08:10,860
The prompt engineering lesson here is
that once you have more than one model,

126
00:08:11,158 --> 00:08:13,156
your prompt is no longer a string.

127
00:08:13,435 --> 00:08:14,508
It's an interface.

128
00:08:14,810 --> 00:08:19,710
You are designing a contract that fi-
that five very different systems have

129
00:08:19,714 --> 00:08:24,878
to honor, and the output format matters
just as much as the instructions matter.

130
00:08:25,282 --> 00:08:29,425
So we proceed the same story with
every one of them, and what we

131
00:08:29,425 --> 00:08:33,643
saw next was the most interesting
part of the whole project.

132
00:08:34,643 --> 00:08:38,358
And wh- which is what happens when
you give the same news story to

133
00:08:38,359 --> 00:08:42,338
five of the best language models
in the world and ask them the same,

134
00:08:42,872 --> 00:08:45,876
carefully written question Excuse me.

135
00:08:46,334 --> 00:08:51,259
Sometimes, they all agree, all five
points at the same sentence and

136
00:08:51,322 --> 00:08:56,334
they say that's loaded language
and that's one-sided sourcing.

137
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:01,544
When that happens, we can f- we can
be fairly confident that bias is real.

138
00:09:02,529 --> 00:09:04,264
But often they don't agree.

139
00:09:04,639 --> 00:09:09,081
Two flag a bias, three don't, or
they all flag, you know, something,

140
00:09:09,133 --> 00:09:11,355
but, they call it different things.

141
00:09:11,670 --> 00:09:13,876
At first, that felt like a failure.

142
00:09:14,366 --> 00:09:18,584
it was like, we, we weren't sure
what to do, but then we realized

143
00:09:18,615 --> 00:09:20,175
it's actually an information.

144
00:09:20,532 --> 00:09:24,584
Disagreement u- usually means
one or, one of the two things.

145
00:09:25,558 --> 00:09:30,417
Either the text is genuinely
ambiguous, reasonable people would

146
00:09:30,475 --> 00:09:36,048
argue about it, or one of the, one
of the model has a blind spot for,

147
00:09:36,246 --> 00:09:38,970
for that type of bias or that topic.

148
00:09:40,136 --> 00:09:43,701
Is-- There was a third issue, consistency.

149
00:09:44,064 --> 00:09:46,849
ask the same model the same
question tomorrow and you may

150
00:09:46,851 --> 00:09:49,209
