short story and show the answers.

229
00:15:19,152 --> 00:15:21,567
By now, they had become something else.

230
00:15:22,177 --> 00:15:23,609
They have become teachers.

231
00:15:23,679 --> 00:15:27,157
Our models learned from
their combined judgment.

232
00:15:27,392 --> 00:15:30,081
Specifically, it learned
from three things.

233
00:15:30,384 --> 00:15:34,757
Where all five LLMs agreed,
that was one indicator.

234
00:15:35,291 --> 00:15:38,242
That label was a strong
training signal for us.

235
00:15:38,696 --> 00:15:43,577
Where they disagreed, it learned from
the expert's decision instead, and

236
00:15:43,620 --> 00:15:48,336
the specialist models acted as an
extra check on everything in between.

237
00:15:48,861 --> 00:15:54,048
In effect, we took the knowledge
spread across five huge general purpose

238
00:15:54,054 --> 00:16:00,223
models, filtered in through human
experts, and concentrated it into one

239
00:16:00,305 --> 00:16:03,102
smaller model that does just one job.

240
00:16:03,643 --> 00:16:05,843
And what does that one model do?

241
00:16:06,376 --> 00:16:12,347
It reads each sentence in the context
of the whole article because a lot of

242
00:16:12,347 --> 00:16:16,207
bias is only visible in, in context.

243
00:16:16,525 --> 00:16:21,848
A sentence can be fine on its
own and loaded next to the one

244
00:16:21,849 --> 00:16:24,296
before it or, or the one after it.

245
00:16:24,590 --> 00:16:30,204
It checks all-- So, so this model
checks all 70 biases in a single pass

246
00:16:30,582 --> 00:16:35,296
instead of five separate API calls
to these large language models, and

247
00:16:35,296 --> 00:16:39,804
it runs on our own infrastructure,
so the data never has to leave.

248
00:16:40,088 --> 00:16:44,226
The next obvious question is: what
does it actually give you back?

249
00:16:44,755 --> 00:16:51,178
Which bring us to my next slide So
here is what the model gives you back.

250
00:16:51,751 --> 00:16:56,886
So take the sentence, "The minister
finally admitted the plan had failed."

251
00:16:57,698 --> 00:17:01,897
Factually, it may be completely
accurate, but look at the words

252
00:17:01,990 --> 00:17:05,240
finally admitted or just admitted.

253
00:17:06,143 --> 00:17:12,792
Admitted suggests the minister was
hiding something, and the word finally

254
00:17:12,955 --> 00:17:16,564
suggests they held out for a long time.

255
00:17:16,968 --> 00:17:20,614
The reader is being nudged
towards a judgment the facts

256
00:17:20,645 --> 00:17:22,310
on their own don't make.

257
00:17:22,696 --> 00:17:26,109
So the model doesn't just,
you know, say biased yes.

258
00:17:26,536 --> 00:17:28,076
It's not just a yes/no model.

259
00:17:28,367 --> 00:17:30,154
It points to the exact words.

260
00:17:30,215 --> 00:17:31,454
It names the bias.

261
00:17:31,919 --> 00:17:33,973
Here, here a loaded verb.

262
00:17:34,438 --> 00:17:38,205
it gives graded severity
because not all bias is…

263
00:17:39,020 --> 00:17:40,496
n-not all bias is equal.

264
00:17:40,866 --> 00:17:44,978
A mild emotive adjective is very
different from, language that

265
00:17:45,027 --> 00:17:46,897
assigns blame without evidence.

266
00:17:47,191 --> 00:17:53,307
And it gives a short reason a human can
read and either agree or disagree with.

267
00:17:53,893 --> 00:17:57,040
Then it can suggest a natural alternative.

268
00:17:57,406 --> 00:18:00,310
The minister said the plan had not worked.

269
00:18:00,729 --> 00:18:06,636
Same facts without the editorial
nudge, and it changes only the biased

270
00:18:06,665 --> 00:18:09,677
part, so the writer's voice is kept.

271
00:18:10,164 --> 00:18:12,275
This was a deliberate design choice.

272
00:18:12,543 --> 00:18:16,339
A score on its own is hard to
trust and impossible to act on.

273
00:18:16,914 --> 00:18:21,692
a highlighted span with a reason is
something a journalist or, editor

274
00:18:21,697 --> 00:18:25,949
can actually use and decide to
ignore, which is just as important.

275
00:18:26,043 --> 00:18:27,748
The human stays in charge.

276
00:18:27,974 --> 00:18:30,470
The model just makes the bias visible.

277
00:18:31,502 --> 00:18:37,174
So which brings to our next
slide, which is why our own model.

278
00:18:37,579 --> 00:18:41,693
So of course, a model of, of
your own is only worth having if

279
00:18:41,694 --> 00:18:43,886
it beats what you already had.

280
00:18:44,161 --> 00:18:47,485
So we benchmark it directly
against our existing pipeline,

281
00:18:47,533 --> 00:18:49,990
the five LLMs with expert prompts.

282
00:18:50,841 --> 00:18:52,648
Three results stood out.

283
00:18:52,674 --> 00:18:53,984
First, consistency.

284
00:18:54,731 --> 00:18:58,731
Large general purpose models can give
different answer to the same story

285
00:18:58,772 --> 00:19:02,520
on different runs, and the five of
them often disagree with each other.

286
00:19:03,303 --> 00:19:06,996
Our model gives the same answer
for the same input every time.

287
00:19:07,398 --> 00:19:13,000
For everyone who wants to measure bias
over time, say comparing how an outlet

288
00:19:13,013 --> 00:19:18,607
covered a topic this month versus the last
month, that repeatability is essential.

289
00:19:19,025 --> 00:19:25,416
You can't measure a trend with a ruler
that changes length Secondly, speed.

290
00:19:26,050 --> 00:19:30,440
The pipeline needed five separate
API calls per story, and you

291
00:19:30,444 --> 00:19:32,483
wait for the slowest one.

292
00:19:32,785 --> 00:19:35,739
Our model does everything in, in one pass.

293
00:19:36,176 --> 00:19:40,162
and third one is, is the cost, a big one.

294
00:19:40,473 --> 00:19:44,910
five paid API calls for every story
adds up fast at news at scale.

295
00:19:44,998 --> 00:19:48,522
Running our model cost fraction of
what we were, paying before that.

296
00:19:49,041 --> 00:19:50,411
now I want to be honest.

297
00:19:50,448 --> 00:19:54,880
This doesn't mean small models
beat big ones at everything.

298
00:19:55,472 --> 00:20:01,274
Ours just does, you know, one narrow
job, but for a narrow job done millions

299
00:20:01,274 --> 00:20:07,440
of times, a specialist trained on,
on, on great data, it will always win.

300
00:20:07,655 --> 00:20:11,117
But, large language model, they
do all sort of things which our

301
00:20:11,138 --> 00:20:14,962
model is not able to do better
than, those large language models

302
00:20:15,962 --> 00:20:21,851
So this brings us to our, the, the final
stage, stage six, the one that never ends.

303
00:20:21,957 --> 00:20:25,739
A bias model is not something
you train once and ship.

304
00:20:26,183 --> 00:20:28,287
Language moves too fast for that.

305
00:20:28,539 --> 00:20:31,746
think about how quickly new
terms appear in the news.

306
00:20:31,816 --> 00:20:36,806
A n- a new conflict, a new election, a
new technology, a new piece of slang,

307
00:20:37,018 --> 00:20:42,465
and with them comes new euphemism, new
loaded labels, new ways of framing people.

308
00:20:42,852 --> 00:20:46,961
A model trained one-- last
year's news will slowly start

309
00:20:46,961 --> 00:20:48,995
to miss the, this year's bias.

310
00:20:49,172 --> 00:20:51,426
So every month, we run the same loop.

311
00:20:51,915 --> 00:20:56,896
One, we take a sample of the model's
recent output on real stories.

312
00:20:57,612 --> 00:21:01,671
Two, our experts analyze
that sample by hand.

313
00:21:02,006 --> 00:21:06,181
the same, careful human review we
used to build the original dataset.

314
00:21:06,639 --> 00:21:09,061
Three, we look for issues and anomalies.

315
00:21:09,361 --> 00:21:11,520
where, where is the model wrong?

316
00:21:11,614 --> 00:21:16,158
Is, is a particular bias suddenly
being over-flagged or under-flagged?

317
00:21:16,591 --> 00:21:19,134
Is there a news topic
where it's struggling?

318
00:21:19,607 --> 00:21:23,031
Is it drifting on a
specific outlet or region?

319
00:21:23,764 --> 00:21:29,668
Four, we fold those th- those findings
back into the dataset, retrain the

320
00:21:29,670 --> 00:21:33,851
model, and benchmark it again before
it replaces the previous version.

321
00:21:34,354 --> 00:21:37,913
So this loop, this loop is
really where the value lives.

322
00:21:38,146 --> 00:21:40,526
The model on day one was good.

323
00:21:40,865 --> 00:21:46,845
The model after months of this loop is
much better, of course, because every

324
00:21:46,847 --> 00:21:51,218
month humans are teaching what is, what
is, what, what it is getting wrong.

325
00:21:51,472 --> 00:21:56,758
I would like to, you know, finish with
four lessons that I think apply well

326
00:21:56,784 --> 00:22:01,894
beyond bias detection to almost any
serious prompt engineering project.

327
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:06,068
Number one is the best prompts
are written by domain experts.

328
00:22:06,704 --> 00:22:11,510
Our biggest quality jumps didn't come
from, you know, clever prompt tricks.

329
00:22:11,851 --> 00:22:13,760
They came from linguists.

330
00:22:14,034 --> 00:22:19,515
linguists who knew precisely what
each bias is and how to describe it.

331
00:22:19,901 --> 00:22:22,902
If you are building in
a specialist fi- spec…

332
00:22:23,430 --> 00:22:26,704
If you are building in a specialist
field, put the specialist

333
00:22:26,722 --> 00:22:28,241
in the prompt writing room.

334
00:22:29,022 --> 00:22:32,078
Second, ask more than one model.

335
00:22:32,625 --> 00:22:37,419
A single LLM gives you an answer
with no sense of how reliable it is.

336
00:22:37,996 --> 00:22:41,023
Five models give you a
distribution or even more.

337
00:22:41,578 --> 00:22:43,967
where they agree, you can move fast.

338
00:22:44,001 --> 00:22:47,204
Where they disagree, you have
found exactly the case that

339
00:22:47,204 --> 00:22:48,611
need a human intervention.

340
00:22:50,157 --> 00:22:53,127
Third, use large models as teachers.

341
00:22:53,487 --> 00:22:58,219
They are incredible at labeling and
reasoning, but expensive and inconsistent

342
00:22:58,230 --> 00:23:00,397
to run on every request forever.

343
00:23:00,982 --> 00:23:06,586
Use them to build a great dataset, then
train a smaller focused model for the job.

344
00:23:07,139 --> 00:23:10,457
It will usually be faster,
cheaper, and more consistent.

345
00:23:11,455 --> 00:23:14,784
And the last one, ship
the loop, not the model.

346
00:23:15,012 --> 00:23:21,052
Model is just a snapshot, and
the loop sample human review

347
00:23:21,726 --> 00:23:24,802
and retrain is the real product.

348
00:23:24,994 --> 00:23:29,811
That's what keeps it accurate as the
world and the language keeps changing.

349
00:23:30,313 --> 00:23:35,488
To, to recap the whole journey in
one sentence, we collected news from

350
00:23:35,523 --> 00:23:41,081
X and Google News, cleaned up, the
duplicates and, and the continuing

351
00:23:41,082 --> 00:23:47,604
stories, asked the five major LLMs the
same expert-designed question, had PhD

352
00:23:47,611 --> 00:23:52,938
linguists and specialist, models to
check the answers, used more than half

353
00:23:52,938 --> 00:23:58,087
a million, balanced stories to train
our own model, and we retrain it every

354
00:23:58,095 --> 00:24:00,413
month with human review, of course.

355
00:24:00,877 --> 00:24:05,864
And the result is a bias detector
that is more consistent, faster, and

356
00:24:05,864 --> 00:24:08,817
cheaper than pipeline we started with.

357
00:24:09,337 --> 00:24:14,896
And I'd argue more trustworthy because
humans are built into every stage of it.

358
00:24:15,328 --> 00:24:16,913
and thank you so much for your time.

359
00:24:16,951 --> 00:24:19,328
I would love to hear your questions.

360
00:24:19,359 --> 00:24:22,044
I have given the, the link to my LinkedIn.

361
00:24:22,230 --> 00:24:26,206
There you can connect with me there,
or you can, take my email from the

362
00:24:26,206 --> 00:24:30,973
organizers and, and definitely I-- if
you are working in the similar, similar

363
00:24:30,973 --> 00:24:32,530
industry, please, connect with me.

364
00:24:32,570 --> 00:24:34,485
I'm always happy to connect nodes.

365
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:35,580
Thank you so much.
